export const meta = {
  name: 'michi-adversarial-review',
  description: 'Adversarial multi-agent review of michi crate: rust practices, architecture, performance, security, lint calibration',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Review' },
    { title: 'Verify' },
  ],
}

const REPO = '/Users/gabe/Projects/michi'

const CONTEXT = `You are reviewing the michi Rust crate (AXI response primitives, the primitive layer of the orin-axi suite) at repo root ${REPO}.

Read CLAUDE.md at the repo root first for non-negotiables. Also read docs/00-overview.md and docs/superpowers/specs/2026-07-03-michi-design.md for the architectural rationale before judging design choices.

WHAT MICHI IS: a pure-computation rendering library -- TOON lists, KV items, truncation, empty states, structured errors, status, hints -- consumed from Rust directly and from TypeScript via a NAPI wrapper (packages/michi-node, npm package "michi"). Four other tools (Monokl, Firkin, Lumen, Pulse) will depend on it. Core design bet: token efficiency plus agent ergonomics without sacrificing human ergonomics. Judge every finding against that bet -- does it serve DX (human maintainers and LLM callers) and long-term maintainability, not just local code taste.

CURRENT SCOPE (Plan 1 / MVP, v0.1.0, COMPLETE -- do not flag missing execution logic as a defect):
- Always-compiled: src/toon/, src/kv/, src/hints.rs, src/truncate.rs, src/empty.rs, src/error.rs, src/idempotency.rs, src/recovery.rs, src/status.rs, src/response.rs, src/resilience/mod.rs, src/pipeline/mod.rs, src/telemetry/mod.rs
- Feature-gated (default = [], zero runtime deps): pipeline (tokio), fuzzy, cache, cli, mcp, napi
- src/pipeline/executor.rs, src/resilience/circuit.rs, src/resilience/policy.rs, src/sink/mod.rs are INTENTIONAL 1-2 line stubs for Plan 2 (not yet implemented). Do NOT flag their absence or emptiness as a defect. DO flag it if the feature-gating or module boundary around them is unsound (e.g. a type leaking outside its feature gate, or a default-feature build accidentally pulling in tokio).
- packages/michi-node is a thin NAPI v3 cdylib shim (#[napi(catch_unwind)] on every export).

NON-NEGOTIABLES from CLAUDE.md (verify actual adherence in the code, do not just take the docs' word for it):
- No unwrap()/expect() in lib code (tests OK with a message)
- No unsafe outside the napi boundary
- BTreeMap for output maps (deterministic ordering), HashMap only for internal state
- Strings pre-allocated with String::with_capacity
- Truncation must use char boundaries, never byte-slice blindly
- Every pub item has a doc comment
- NAPI: napi/napi-derive = "3", #[napi(catch_unwind)] on every export, no u64 across the FFI boundary
- Benchmarks use divan, tests use cargo nextest

LINT POSTURE already in place (src/lib.rs, .clippy.toml, .rustfmt.toml): clippy::pedantic plus a curated slice of clippy::restriction (unwrap_used, expect_used, panic, todo, unimplemented, indexing_slicing, disallowed_macros, implicit_clone, inefficient_to_string, use_self) warned in the lib crate; several of those are DENIED in packages/michi-node (panics at the FFI boundary crash the host Node process). 120-char width. This was tuned to be "pedantic as a baseline, minus rules that fight good structure."

Also actively look for OVER-engineering or premature abstraction in the other direction: ceremony that does not add real safety or clarity. The goal is to lint consistently without making it hard to write the best possible code, not maximal strictness for its own sake.

Be adversarial: actively try to find real, concrete defects, not style nits. For every finding, give an exact file:line and a concrete failure scenario -- specific inputs or state that produce a wrong result, a panic, or a real (not theoretical) maintenance hazard. Do not report a finding unless you can name the concrete trigger. Do not flag things that are explicitly intentional per the scope notes above.`

const DIMENSIONS = [
  {
    key: 'rust-practices',
    prompt: `${CONTEXT}

DIMENSION: Rust best-practices adherence.

Read every file under src/ (all .rs files, about 1900 lines total -- this is a small crate, read all of it, not excerpts) and packages/michi-node/src/lib.rs. Check:
- Every non-negotiable above is actually followed in the code, not just declared in CLAUDE.md or the lib.rs lint attributes -- grep for unwrap(), expect(, panic!, unsafe, HashMap, BTreeMap yourself and inspect each hit's context (test module vs lib code).
- Idiomatic error handling: does michi::Error's design (thiserror, ErrorClass, is_retryable) hold up? Is any variant's #[error(...)] message, class() mapping, or is_retryable() mapping wrong or inconsistent with the others?
- API ergonomics: builder patterns (AgentResponse, StatusResponse, RecoveryHint), trait design, any surprising ownership/borrowing choices, any pub item genuinely missing a doc comment despite the #![warn(missing_docs)] lint.
- For every function with a doc comment describing an invariant or a bound, read the doc comment AND the function body together and check whether the code actually satisfies what the comment promises. Do not trust the comment at face value.
- Any place where a #[allow(clippy::...)] suppression's justification comment is factually wrong given what the code actually does.

Return findings via the schema. Severity: critical (data corruption, panic, or UB), high (a real bug triggered by realistic input), medium (a contract violation with no easy trigger yet, or a discoverable footgun), low (a minor idiom deviation with a concrete, non-hypothetical downside).`,
  },
  {
    key: 'architecture',
